PLACES=[
N('ss-moyie-kaslo','S.S. Moyie','Kaslo','bc','landmark','standing',49.91172,-116.90236,'exact','open','walk','','1898–1957',
  'A Canadian Pacific Railway sternwheeler built at the company\'s Nelson shipyard and launched on October 22, 1898, with a steel frame and machinery from Bertram of Toronto and a hull planked in wood. She ran across Kootenay Lake to Kootenay Landing as the "Crow boat", meeting the trains of the new Crowsnest Pass line, then from 1906 carried freight and excursions. Retired in 1957, she was sold to the then City of Kaslo for one dollar and in July 1958 was pulled ashore on Kaslo Bay to become a museum. She was made a National Historic Site in 1958; the Kootenay Lake Historical Society calls her the oldest known intact passenger sternwheeler in the world.',
  'The whole ship, dry-docked on the south shore of Kaslo Bay: hull, superstructure, smoke stack, the 5.9-metre stern paddle wheel with its 17 buckets, hog posts and chains, capstan, the dining saloon and cabins, and the original steam whistle, which visitors may blow, weather permitting (Register statement and the society\'s page). Also on the grounds: a CPR caboose, the ladies\' saloon of the Kuskanook and a miner\'s cabin. Only the freight deck is wheelchair accessible.',
  'A one-dollar sternwheeler that never left the shore again.',
  'Owned by the Village of Kaslo (sold to the city in 1958) and run by the Kootenay Lake Historical Society, at 324 Front Street. Open from Mother\'s Day in May to Canadian Thanksgiving weekend, 10 am to 5 pm daily; admission $12 adult, $10 senior or student, $5 child 6 to 14 (society\'s page, read November 2026). The entrance doubles as the Kaslo visitor centre, with public washrooms. '+KTOWN,
  [CR(12748,'S.S. Moyie National Historic Site of Canada'),W('Moyie (sternwheeler)'),['Kootenay Lake Historical Society: plan your visit','https://www.klhs.bc.ca/plan-your-visit.html'],['Kootenay Lake Historical Society: about the S.S. Moyie','https://www.klhs.bc.ca/s.s.-moyie.html']],brick='no'),
N('kaslo-municipal-hall','Kaslo Municipal Hall','Kaslo','bc','civic','standing',49.91061,-116.90484,'exact','view','walk','','1898',
  'Kaslo\'s town hall, built in 1898 at the height of the Kootenay silver boom, five years after the town began, to the design of Ewart and Carrie of Nelson; D.J. McLachlan built it. It held the council chamber and offices, the provincial court, the fire and police departments and the jail under one roof. It was made a National Historic Site in 1984 as the oldest remaining town hall on the British Columbia mainland, and it is still the municipal hall.',
  'A two-storey wooden hall on a rubble-stone basement with brick corner quoins, with a double curved front staircase, a two-storey columned portico and a belfry with open arcades on top, the bell still in it. The Register lists original interior work: the front hall and staircase, the vault, the second-floor court room and chambers, lath and plaster, wainscoting and old inscriptions on the doors. Public hours of the village office were not checked, so whether the court room can be seen was not checked.',
  'The oldest town hall on the BC mainland, with a jail and a court upstairs.',
  'Owned and used by the Village of Kaslo, on Fourth Street. Seen from the street at any time; office hours not checked. '+KTOWN,
  [CR(12065,'Kaslo Municipal Hall National Historic Site of Canada'),CR(19024,'Kaslo Municipal Hall')],brick='?'),
N('langham-kaslo','The Langham','Kaslo','bc','hotel','standing',49.91151,-116.90673,'exact','open','walk','','1896',
  'A three-storey wooden hotel of 1896, built by Chas. J. Kapps, and the only hotel of Kaslo\'s mining boom and bust still standing in its original form. Over the years it was a boarding house, a bank, a bottling works and a lumber office. From 1942 Japanese Canadians removed from the coast were interned in Kaslo, and the building housed internees. It was due to be pulled down in the early 1970s; local people formed the Langham Cultural Society and restored it, and it was made a provincial heritage site in 1977.',
  'Ten dormer windows, a parapet front with a cornice, and (the Register says) the original stained glass on the front and back, the original central staircase and original door and window trim inside. The Japanese Canadian Museum inside tells the internment story and keeps a sleeping room laid out as the Konno family\'s room was. The museum page says self-guided access is open year round, 7 days a week, 9 am to 6 pm, admission by donation.',
  'A boom-town hotel that became a home for interned families, then a theatre.',
  'Owned and run by the Langham Cultural Society, at 447 A Avenue. Museum and building open 9 am to 6 pm daily, by donation (the Langham\'s page and the FETCH directory, read November 2026). '+KTOWN,
  [CR(19033,'Langham Cultural Centre'),['The Langham: Japanese Canadian Museum','https://thelangham.ca/japanese-canadian-museum'],['FETCH directory: Langham Cultural Centre',FETCH+'1246']],brick='no'),
N('st-marks-anglican-kaslo','St. Mark\'s Anglican Church','Kaslo','bc','worship','standing',49.90999,-116.9084,'exact','view','walk','','1895',
  'Kaslo\'s second-oldest church, built in 1895 by volunteer labour; Mary Buchanan, wife of the sawmill owner George Buchanan, gave the lumber. Services have been held in it for over a hundred years. A plaque in it remembers six miners who died in 1910.',
  'A small wooden Victorian church at the foot of a wooded hillside, with bevelled wood siding, a cedar shake roof and a bell tower whose bell still rings. Inside, the Register describes hammer-beam trusses under a vaulted ceiling (the only ones in Kaslo), the original pews, pulpit, wainscoting and trim, a pump organ, and original stained-glass windows, two of the eight replaced in 1938 by pictorial windows from England. A labyrinth in the woods behind it is open to the community. Whether visitors can go in outside services was not checked.',
  '',
  'An active Anglican church at 601 Fifth Street. Seen from the street; service times not checked. '+KTOWN,
  [CR(19027,'St. Mark\'s Anglican Church')],brick='no'),
N('st-andrews-united-kaslo','St. Andrew\'s United Church','Kaslo','bc','worship','standing',49.91003,-116.90468,'exact','view','walk','','1893',
  'Kaslo\'s first church, built in 1893 as Robertson Presbyterian Church and believed to be one of the oldest churches in British Columbia in continuous use. Before its interior was finished it sheltered people burned out by the 1894 fire and then flooded out the same year. From 1942 to 1945 it shared its services with the Japanese Canadian mission under Rev. K. Shimizu. The congregation joined Kaslo\'s Methodists in 1925.',
  'A white wooden Gothic church with a spire-topped belfry and a cedar shingle roof; the bell, bought in 1896 as the village\'s alarm, is still used. The Register says the original flooring, pews, the first Estey organ and a pot-bellied stove remain inside, with the organ, pulpit and minister\'s chair from the 1895 Methodist church. Whether visitors can go in outside services and events was not checked.',
  '',
  'An active United church at 500 Fourth Street, corner of B Avenue; its basement houses a community food project. Seen from the street; service times not checked. '+KTOWN,
  [CR(19029,'St. Andrew\'s United Church')],brick='no'),
N('sacred-heart-kaslo','Sacred Heart Catholic Church','Kaslo','bc','worship','standing',49.91208,-116.90696,'exact','view','walk','','1902',
  'A modest Catholic church built in 1902, where services have been held ever since.',
  'A white wooden church with Gothic windows of clear and stained glass and its original wooden pews (Register statement), set in grassy grounds against the forest. Whether visitors can go in outside services was not checked.',
  '',
  'An active Catholic church at 313 Fifth Street. Seen from the street; service times not checked. '+KTOWN,
  [CR(19028,'Sacred Heart Catholic Church')],brick='no'),
N('kaslo-1896-building','The 1896 Building','Kaslo','bc','town','standing',49.91134,-116.90296,'exact','view','walk','','1896',
  'A two-storey brick store built in 1896 for Riedel and Kuester, bakers and grocers, and one of the first buildings made of brick from the Millington Brothers\' brick and tile yard in Kaslo. It has since held a church and a run of shops.',
  'Red brick with arched window heads, corbelled bullnose brick, a tie-bar, two wooden pillars at the door, two big shop windows, a separate street door to the upper floor, and an "1896" sign on the front (Register statement; the red brick from the Register\'s photograph). Shops inside; interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Privately owned commercial building at 331 Front Street. Seen from the street; open when the shops are. '+KTOWN,
  [CR(19034,'1896 Building')],brick='yes'),
N('kaslo-masonic-hall','Kaslo Masonic Hall','Kaslo','bc','civic','standing',49.91016,-116.90204,'exact','view','walk','','1894',
  'Built as a store for the merchants the Green brothers after fire took their first one; tax records first show it in 1894. The second storey was added in 1897 for $1,200 as the lodge room of Kaslo Lodge No. 25, which has met there since 1897; the lodge took over the building in 1938. Kaslo\'s first post office was at the back, and during the internment the Japanese Canadian doctor Shimo Takahara and the dentist Dr. Banno practised on the ground floor. The Register calls it the oldest wooden Masonic hall in use in British Columbia.',
  'A two-storey wooden building on the corner of A Avenue and Third Street, now under synthetic siding, with a large Masonic emblem on its north face. The lodge room upstairs is described as nearly unchanged; it is not open to the public as far as any source says. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Owned by the Kaslo Masonic Holding Society, at 301 A Avenue. View from the street only. '+KTOWN,
  [CR(24576,'Kaslo Masonic Hall')],brick='no'),
N('nikkei-internment-memorial-centre','Nikkei Internment Memorial Centre','New Denver','bc','inst','standing',49.98658,-117.37503,'exact','open','walk','','1942–',
  'In 1942 the federal government built an internment camp for Japanese Canadians removed from the coast on an orchard and vegetable ground in New Denver known as the Orchard: by that year 1,505 people lived in 275 shacks they built themselves. Unlike most camps it was not torn down after the war; former internees were deeded their homes and lots, and some stayed. The memorial centre was made in 1992 to 1994 to keep the buildings, and it was named a National Historic Site in 2007.',
  'Inside a tall wooden fence with two front gates: three original internment shacks among five buildings, kept with their stoves and furnishings, a Buddhist sanctuary, personal belongings given by the community, interpretive panels, and a Japanese garden designed by Roy (Tomomichi) Sumi. Round it, in the Orchard, some rebuilt shacks still stand as homes; those are private.',
  'One of the few internment camps that was never pulled down.',
  'A museum at 306 Josephine Street, open May 1 to September 30, 7 days a week, 10 am to 5 pm (the FETCH directory, updated 2026; Wikipedia gives the same season). Admission not checked. The houses of the Orchard round it are private homes. Parking was not checked on the ground.',
  [CR(15382,'Nikkei Internment Memorial Centre National Historic Site of Canada'),CR(23036,'Nikkei Internment Memorial Centre'),W('Nikkei Internment Memorial Centre'),W('New Denver'),['FETCH directory: Nikkei Internment Memorial Centre',FETCH+'1235']],brick='no'),
N('new-denver-church','New Denver Church','New Denver','bc','worship','standing',49.99277,-117.37586,'site','view','walk','','1893',
  'The first church built in New Denver, in 1893. It was later turned to other uses: it became the Lakeview Collegiate high school, served again for worship, and was a gathering place for the Japanese Canadians interned in the town (Register statement for the New Denver internment places).',
  'A church building on Josephine Street. The Register statement lists no character-defining elements and no description of the building, and its present use was not found. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Owner and present use not checked; seen from Josephine Street only. Parking was not checked on the ground.',
  [CR(23242,'New Denver Church')],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('silvery-slocan-museum','Silvery Slocan Museum (former Bank of Montreal)','New Denver','bc','town','standing',49.99168,-117.37663,'exact','open','walk','','1896',
  'The former Bank of Montreal on Sixth Avenue, built during the Slocan silver boom, when New Denver on the lake and the railway was the supply town for the mines round Sandon. A Waymarking write-up gives the year as 1896 and calls it the first Bank of Montreal built in British Columbia; Kootenay Rockies Tourism describes the vault as an 1897 brick bank vault. The building year is from that one write-up and was not confirmed in an official source.',
  'A museum of the Slocan\'s mining, logging and farm history. The main floor keeps the 1897 brick bank vault, business machines and things from the New Market Hotel; upstairs are a Victorian bedroom and kitchen, furnishings from the early Slocan Community Hospital and pieces from the old Knox Church. Outside is the restored motor launch Lancet and displays on the Slocan Lake boats (Kootenay Rockies Tourism).',
  '',
  'Run by the Silvery Slocan Historical Society at 202 Sixth Avenue, which also houses the visitor centre. Open weekends from the May Day weekend to July 1, then daily to Labour Day, 10 am to 5 pm in July and August; admission by donation (Kootenay Rockies Tourism; the Village of New Denver\'s own page answered 403 and was not read). Parking was not checked on the ground.',
  [['Kootenay Rockies Tourism: Silvery Slocan Museum','https://www.kootenayrockies.com/partner/silvery-slocan-museum'],['Slocan Valley directory: Silvery Slocan Museum','https://slocanvalley.com/?p=10238'],['Waymarking: Bank of Montreal, New Denver','https://www.waymarking.com/waymarks/WMJY8B_Bank_of_Montreal_New_Denver_BC']],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('slocan-mercantile-sandon','Slocan Mercantile Building (Sandon Museum)','Sandon','bc','town','standing',49.97597,-117.22736,'site','open','drive','','1900',
  'Sandon, high in the Carpenter Creek valley, was the boom town of the Slocan silver strikes of the 1890s. A fire in May 1900 destroyed most of the town centre, and this two-storey brick store was built in the rebuilding that followed; the Register calls it a rare commercial building of that time. During the Second World War Japanese Canadians interned at Sandon lived in it, and new windows were cut in its east wall in the 1940s. A flood in 1955 destroyed most of what was left of the town. Since the 1970s volunteers have restored it, and it holds the Sandon Historical Society\'s museum.',
  'Red brick walls on a stone foundation, a finer faced-brick front with a moulded cornice, a lightly decorated parapet with sawtooth brickwork, a central front porch and the storefront openings, facing the line of the old timber flume that carried Carpenter Creek under Main Street (Register statement; the red brick from the Register\'s photograph). Inside, the museum.',
  'The one brick store of a silver town that burned, flooded and emptied.',
  'The Sandon Historical Society\'s museum, on Slocan Star Street. Open from the May long weekend to Thanksgiving, daily; the FETCH directory gives 9 am to 5 pm and the Sandon visitor site 10 am to 5 pm, with a $5 admission. Sandon is 5.6 km up the Sandon road from Highway 31A, about 8 km east of New Denver; parking was not checked on the ground. Much of the townsite is privately owned: keep to the streets and open buildings.',
  [CR(21056,'Slocan Mercantile General Store'),W('Sandon, British Columbia'),['FETCH directory: Sandon Historical Society Museum',FETCH+'1236'],SANDON],conf='mixed',brick='yes'),
N('sandon-city-hall','Sandon City Hall','Sandon','bc','civic','standing',49.97613,-117.22962,'exact','open','drive','','1900',
  'Sandon\'s city hall on Carpenter Creek, rebuilt in 1900 after the town fire, with a courtroom, a jail and a fire hall. The city went bankrupt and was disincorporated in 1920; the hall became a school in 1925 and stayed one until 1954 (Sandon visitor site). Wikipedia names it among the pioneer buildings the townsite\'s owners have worked to keep.',
  'The 1900 hall, now a small cafe and gift shop, the Prospector\'s Pick, with a new museum room in its east and west wings (Sandon visitor site). What original fabric survives inside was not checked.',
  '',
  'Privately run cafe and gift shop on Slocan Star Street. The visitor site gives 10 am to 5 pm daily for the 2024 season (May 18 to October 14); hours for 2026 not checked. Parking was not checked on the ground.',
  [SANDON,W('Sandon, British Columbia')],conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('silversmith-powerhouse-sandon','Silversmith Powerhouse','Sandon','bc','industry','standing',49.97507,-117.22564,'site','open','drive','','1897–',
  'A hydroelectric plant that began in 1897 as the Slocan Star plant and still runs, now supplying Sandon and the New Denver area. The Sandon visitor site says Sandon was the first place in British Columbia with an electric utility open to every citizen, and that eight hydro systems worked round the town at its peak. Wikipedia calls it the second hydro plant in Western Canada after Nelson\'s and the oldest one still producing; its machinery was first installed in 1905 at a plant in Vernon and moved to Sandon in 1916.',
  'A working powerhouse with its old generating machinery, shown on free tours by the family company that owns it, Silversmith Power and Light. Outside on Slocan Star Street stands CPR steam locomotive 6947, built in 1908, brought to Sandon in 1997 and viewable at any time, and a row of old Brill trolley buses.',
  'A silver-boom powerhouse that has not stopped since 1897.',
  'Owned by Silversmith Power and Light. Tours free (donations welcome), 10 am to 5 pm daily in season; the visitor site gives May 18 to October 14, 2024, and tours by appointment in winter. Hours for 2026 not checked. Parking was not checked on the ground.',
  [SANDON,W('Sandon, British Columbia')],brick='?'),
N('payne-bluff-ks-trail','Payne Bluff and the Kaslo & Slocan grade','Sandon','bc','rail','traces',50.00541,-117.25786,'area','public','hike','','1895–1910',
  'The Kaslo and Slocan Railway, a narrow-gauge line financed by the Great Northern, reached Sandon from Kaslo in October 1895 to carry Slocan silver ore to the lake. West of Sandon it kept nearly level and ran across Payne Bluff, more than 800 feet above Three Forks. A forest fire in 1910 burned the line\'s bridges and snowsheds; the narrow-gauge equipment was gone by 1914. The grade from Sandon to the bluff is now a hiking trail.',
  'The trail (about 13.6 km there and back from the Sandon trailhead, by West Kootenay Hiking) passes the signed Altoona Mine site, the ruins of the Payne concentrator at about 3.7 km with an interpretive sign, where the path crosses big tailing piles, and the tailings of the Victor Mine across the valley; old ties and rails show in the grade. A signed junction at 6.2 km leads up to the bluff, where rockfall blocks parts of the path. The pin is a point on the old grade, not the concentrator.',
  'Ore trains on a ledge eight hundred feet up.',
  'A signed trail with kiosks at the Sandon end (on Reco Road above Sandon) and on Highway 31A 0.8 km past the Sandon road (the Three Forks end, 1.7 km below the bluff junction). Who owns the land under the trail was not checked. Season mid-April to late November by the hiking write-up; no managing body\'s page was found, so rules and closures were not checked. The trail is narrow at the tailing piles and above long drops.',
  [W('Kaslo and Slocan Railway'),['West Kootenay Hiking: Payne Bluffs (K&S Trail)','https://westkootenayhiking.ca/payne-bluffs-ks-trail/'],W('Three Forks, British Columbia')],conf='mixed',th=[50.01245,-117.28201],thn='Highway 31A lot near the Three Forks trailhead',brick='?'),
N('three-forks-bc','Three Forks','Three Forks','bc','town','traces',50.01667,-117.28333,'area','public','walk','','1892–1920s',
  'A mining camp at the meeting of Carpenter, Seaton and Kane creeks, on the pack-train route from the mines to New Denver. The Nakusp and Slocan Railway reached it in October 1894, with a turntable and a two-stall roundhouse, and by January 1895 it had stores, hotels, a drugstore and a jail. When the railways went on to Sandon in 1895 Sandon grew at its expense; by 1918 only the store was left.',
  'An interpretive sign in a clearing; what is left of the town lies hidden in the undergrowth round it (Wikipedia). The Galena Trail, on the old Nakusp and Slocan grade, passes through the site.',
  '',
  'The Galena Trail (Rosebery to Three Forks Regional Trail) belongs to the Regional District of Central Kootenay: 13 km of the old CPR right-of-way for walking and cycling, with trailheads at Rosebery, at the turn-off to Sandon and on Denver Siding Road. Hours not given on the district\'s page. A flower shop and farm work at the road junction: keep off their ground.',
  [W('Three Forks, British Columbia'),['Regional District of Central Kootenay: Galena Trail','https://rdck.ca/community-services/galena-trail/']],th=[50.01087,-117.28181],thn='Galena Trail trailhead, Sandon Cody Road',brick='no'),
]

